# Cloudflare Workers Assets — headers for files served directly (no Worker;
# see wrangler.toml run_worker_first). Responses the Worker builds itself —
# redirects, 404, /api/*, /embed/* — set their own Cache-Control in src/index.js.
#
# Cache-Control — the buckets the Worker applied to every file until
# 2026-10-01, except that `?v=<hash>` files drop from 1 year to 1 hour:
# _headers matches paths, never query strings, so it can't tell them apart
# from the ~15 Worker scripts (mergeWorker.js, pdfEncrypt.js, …) that are
# loaded without `?v=` and must not be cached for a year (stale code after a
# deploy). Revalidation after the hour is a cheap 304, and free.
#   /js /css /fonts /icons + five root files: 1 hour
#   everything else — every HTML page, sw.js: no rule, so Cloudflare's own
#   default "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate" (revalidate each time, same
#   effect as no-cache)
# The 1-hour rules deliberately never overlap a path that can be HTML: two
# matching rules JOIN their values ("no-cache, public, max-age=3600" — seen on
# the real preview for "/"), and `! Cache-Control` detaching didn't prevent it.

# Global security headers applied to every response
# Permissions-Policy: camera=(self) — the Document Scanner tool's
# live-camera flow (js/scanCameraUI.js, getUserMedia) needs camera
# access on pdfree.io's own pages; (self) still blocks any third-party
# embed of this site from ever requesting it. Real production-only bug
# found via Playwright during the 2026-08-22 QA pass: this header can't
# be set via a <meta> tag (unlike CSP), so it never showed up testing
# against a local dist/ server (which sends no such header at all) —
# worked locally, silently failed camera access in production until
# fixed here. (Originally added for jpg2pdf's own camera-scan flow,
# since folded into the dedicated Document Scanner tool and reverted out
# of jpg2pdf — this header stays, now serving Document Scanner instead.)
# microphone/geolocation stay fully disabled — nothing uses them.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security below is NOT what's actually served — verified
# live 2026-08-24, response sends max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains, no
# preload. Cloudflare's own dashboard-level HSTS zone setting (SSL/TLS →
# Edge Certificates → HSTS) overrides this specific header at the edge,
# after _headers/Workers has already set it; every OTHER header on this
# block (X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options, Referrer-Policy,
# Permissions-Policy) is confirmed live-matching, so this is specific to
# HSTS having its own dedicated Cloudflare feature, not _headers being
# generally ignored. The value below is the intended target — to actually
# apply it (notably `preload`, which is required for HSTS preload-list
# eligibility), change the dashboard setting directly; editing this line
# alone won't take effect.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload

/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/robots.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/sitemap.xml
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
  Content-Type: application/xml; charset=utf-8

/version.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/js/vendor/qpdf/lib/qpdf.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm
